{"attack":true,"target_alias":"SRC_001","cited_input_aliases":["SRC_001"],"claim":"The proposal conflates defense chains with skeptical grounded (IN) status, so for the stated threshold d (above the longest defense chain) the seeding labels unattacked conjectures that are non-winnable IN, which must later be flipped to OUT, violating the stipulated monotone seeding and thus the very guarantee the proposal adds to preserve the fixed point.","grounds":"The target defines d as 'above the longest defense chain in the polynomial-sized graph.' If d exceeds that chain length, then under the natural reading of 'defense chain,' every argument has a defense chain within the bound—not only the winnable ones. The target then claims seeding 'coincides with the standard' grounded extension and is 'monotone (never flips IN to OUT later).' But grounded IN status is skeptical fixed-point status, not mere existence of a defense chain: an unattacked but non-winnable conjecture would be seeded IN by this bound yet must be OUT in the standard grounded extension. That is exactly the prohibited IN→OUT flip. So the strengthening does not preserve determinism/reinstatement/root validity as stated, because the claimed monotonicity is false unless a winnability criterion is supplied—a scope the target never specifies.","counterexample":[{"graph":"A (unattacked); B (unattacked); C attacked by B; A defends C via B; C attacks A","note":"A has a defense chain of length 2 (A defends itself against C, who is defeated by defeating B), within the bound d above the longest chain. But the grounded extension is {B}, since A and C mutually attack and cannot both be IN; A is not skeptically accepted.","flaw":"Seeding by 'has a defense chain up to d' marks A IN. Since A is OUT in the standard grounded extension, A must later flip IN→OUT, contradicting the claimed monotone seeding."}]}